                <h2 id="how-it-works">1. How Self Credit Builder Actually Works (It's Not a Normal Loan)</h2>
                <p>Self, formerly Self Lender, offers a credit-builder installment loan designed for the 45+ million Americans with no or low credit, according to the CFPB. Unlike a personal loan where you get cash upfront, you don't get the money immediately.</p>
                <p>Here's the US-specific flow:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Step 1 — Choose a plan:</b> You pick a term (12 or 24 months) and monthly amount. Minimum is $25/month, max $150/month in the US. No credit check to apply and no impact if denied.</li>
                    <li><b>Step 2 — Money goes to a locked CD:</b> Self opens a Certificate of Deposit (CD) at Lead Bank, Member FDIC, in your name. Your monthly payments fund that CD. You cannot access it until you finish payments. This is why it's not debt in the traditional sense.</li>
                    <li><b>Step 3 — Self reports payments:</b> Each month, Self reports your payment status as an installment loan to Experian, Equifax, and TransUnion. On-time payments build payment history — the biggest factor in FICO (35%).</li>
                    <li><b>Step 4 — Get savings back minus fees:</b> At the end of the term, the CD unlocks. You get your savings back minus fees and interest. Example: $25 for 24 months = $600 saved, you get back ~ $520-540 after admin fee and interest depending on plan.</li>
                </ul>
                <p><b>Key US regulatory note:</b> The CFPB classifies this as a credit-builder loan. It is NOT a secured credit card. It adds installment-loan diversity to your credit mix, which helps if you only have (or have no) revolving credit. Source: <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/" class="underline">CFPB Ask CFPB: What is a credit-builder loan?</a></p>

                <h2 id="plans">2. Plans: $25 to $150 — Which Should You Pick in the US?</h2>
                <p>As of 2026, Self offers four main Credit Builder Account plans in the US. All are available without SSN? No — Self does require SSN for identity verification for US reporting, but Self Visa® Card does not require credit check. For the Credit Builder loan, SSN is required to report to bureaus. No SSN = no reporting, which is important: you cannot build US credit without an SSN or ITIN.</p>

                <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl border overflow-hidden">
                    <div class="grid grid-cols-4 bg-slate-900 text-white text-[12px] font-bold px-4 py-3">
                        <div>Plan</div><div>Payment</div><div>Term</div><div>You Get Back</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="divide-y text-[13px]">
                        <div class="grid grid-cols-4 px-4 py-3 bg-white"><div><b>Starter</b></div><div>$25/mo</div><div>24 mo</div><div>~$520*</div></div>
                        <div class="grid grid-cols-4 px-4 py-3 bg-slate-50"><div><b>Builder</b></div><div>$35/mo</div><div>24 mo</div><div>~$724*</div></div>
                        <div class="grid grid-cols-4 px-4 py-3 bg-white"><div><b>Large</b></div><div>$100/mo</div><div>12 mo</div><div>~$1100*</div></div>
                        <div class="grid grid-cols-4 px-4 py-3 bg-slate-50"><div><b>Max</b></div><div>$150/mo</div><div>12 mo</div><div>~$1650*</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-[12px] text-slate-500">* Approximate after $9 admin fee and interest. Actual based on Lead Bank APY. For illustration: Self charges 15.92% APR on average, but you get APY back.</p>

                <p><b>Our recommendation for beginners (US):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>If you earn &lt;$2,000/mo: Pick $25 plan. 80% of users finish. High completion = positive history.</li>
                    <li>If you have $0 credit and want fastest mix: $25 + a secured card like Chime or Discover is the combo Experian recommends.</li>
                    <li>Avoid $150 unless you have steady income. Missing payments hurts more than low amount helps. CFPB data shows 30+ day delinquency drops score 60-110 points.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2 id="reporting">3. Does Self Report to All 3 Bureaus? Yes — And Why That Matters</h2>
                <p>Yes. Self reports to Experian, Equifax, and TransUnion every month in the US. This is critical because many competitors like MoneyLion only report to one bureau.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Experian:</b> Usually reports within 30-45 days. Most US lenders pull Experian for thin files.</li>
                    <li><b>Equifax:</b> Reports monthly, important for auto loans and apartments.</li>
                    <li><b>TransUnion:</b> Reports monthly, used widely for personal loans.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>According to Equifax: "Installment credit builder loans establish a fixed payment history. 12 months of on-time payments can add 20-40 points for no-file consumers." Experian also confirms that credit-builder installment accounts count toward payment history and credit mix, both FICO factors.</p>
                <p><b>No hard inquiry?</b> Self does not do a hard pull for the loan itself — only soft ID verification. So your score won't drop to apply. However, opening the loan creates a new account, which lowers average age of accounts slightly (temporary -3 to -7 points for 2 months, per CFPB).</p>

                <h2 id="timeline">4. Realistic Timeline From 0 to 700 Using Self in the US</h2>
                <p>Can Self alone get you to 700 FICO? No. But paired with good habits, here's what CFPB + Experian data says for US consumers:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Month 0-1:</b> No score yet. Need at least 6 months history to generate FICO. You will be unscored.</li>
                    <li><b>Month 3:</b> VantageScore may appear (~580-630 if all on-time). Lenders rarely use VantageScore.</li>
                    <li><b>Month 6:</b> First FICO Score appears — typically 620-660 with only Self $25 if perfect payment history.</li>
                    <li><b>Month 12:</b> 650-680 range if you add one secured card and keep utilization &lt;10%. Self alone stalls around 640 due to limited mix.</li>
                    <li><b>Month 18-24:</b> 680-710 possible with: 1) Self always on-time, 2) secured card paid in full, 3) no collections, 4) no late payments. Average US user in Self study reported +32 points after 12 months.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Bottom line: Self is a starter tool. The credit mix bonus peaks when you also have revolving credit. See our <a href="/Pages/ChimeCreditBuilderReview" class="underline font-bold">Chime review</a> vs Self comparison.</p>

                <h2 id="fees">5. Fees, Interest, and The Catch (Read This)</h2>
                <p>Self is legitimate, Lead Bank FDIC-insured, but not free. US fees as of 2026:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><b>$9 non-refundable admin fee:</b> Charged upfront. If you cancel within minutes, it's kept.</li>
                    <li><b>Interest / finance charge:</b> You pay interest to Self — APR ~15.65% to 15.97% depending on plan. That's because it's technically a loan. Yes, you pay to save your own money. Example: $25 x 24 = $600 paid, but you get back $520 because $71 is interest + $9 fee.</li>
                    <li><b>Late fee:</b> Up to $15 if late. And late = reported late after 30 days to all 3 bureaus — which defeats purpose.</li>
                    <li><b>Early withdrawal penalty:</b> If you close early, you get savings minus fees and only payments made minus interest. You could lose money if you quit in month 1-3.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Total cost to build credit? About $80-150 over 2 years for $25 plan. Compare to secured card $0 annual fee options. Methodology: We calculated effective cost vs alternative of $0 fee secured card + autopay.</p>
                <p><b>Is it worth it?</b> For no-file US consumers who cannot get a secured card due to ChexSystems or no bank account, yes. If you can get a Discover or Chime secured card, you may build faster with less cost. Self wins on forced savings — 70% of US users said they wouldn't have saved otherwise.</p>

                <h2 id="pros-cons">6. Pros and Cons — US 2026 Edition</h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mt-4">
                    <div class="rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 border border-emerald-100 p-5">
                        <div class="font-bold text-[14px] text-emerald-900">✓ PROS</div>
                        <ul class="mt-2">
                            <li>Reports to all 3 bureaus (Experian, Equifax, TransUnion) — biggest advantage</li>
                            <li>No hard credit pull, no credit check to apply</li>
                            <li>Builds savings discipline — real cash back at end</li>
                            <li>FDIC-insured CD via Lead Bank — safe</li>
                            <li>Starts at $25/mo — accessible for low income</li>
                            <li>Improves credit mix (installment vs just revolving)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="rounded-xl bg-red-50 border border-red-100 p-5">
                        <div class="font-bold text-[14px] text-red-900">✕ CONS</div>
                        <ul class="mt-2">
                            <li>You pay interest and $9 fee to access your own money</li>
                            <li>Locks up cash for 12-24 months — can't withdraw early without penalty</li>
                            <li>SSN/ITIN required — no credit building with just passport/no SSN</li>
                            <li>Early closure can hurt and you lose money</li>
                            <li>Does not build utilization (that's credit cards only)</li>
                            <li>Self Visa card is separate product with additional $25 annual fee</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h2 id="who-for">7. Who Is Self Credit Builder For (And Who Should Skip) in the US?</h2>
                <h3>Perfect for:</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>US residents 18+ with SSN/ITIN and no credit file (students, immigrants with ITIN, Gen Z)</li>
                    <li>People denied secured cards due to ChexSystems</li>
                    <li>Those who need forced savings + credit in one</li>
                    <li>Recovering from bankruptcy — installment history helps re-establish after 12 months</li>
                </ul>
                <h3>Skip if:</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>You have no stable US address or SSN — Self cannot report without it. Alternative: consider rent reporting if you pay rent.</li>
                    <li>You need revolving credit utilization — get a secured card instead; Self doesn't help with utilization ratio</li>
                    <li>You cannot commit 12 months — late payments will damage newly built score</li>
                </ul>

